      "fact": 1,
      "headline": "The March to Moat Cailin",
      "text": "The camp at Winterfell rose in a clamor of leather creaking and iron striking stone. The road to the south was dry and hard, the dust kicking up in long plumes that caught the pale late-summer sun. Lord Jon Umber stood atop his horse, his eyes scanning the columns of six thousand men—Umber, Glover, Bolton, Norrey, and Liddle—until the dust swallowed them. Roose Bolton rode near the front, his face a mask of cold stone; he said nothing, only watched the road ahead. The banners snapped in the wind, and the silence of the march grew heavy as they turned their backs to the Keep.",
      "pov": "Jon Umber"
    },
    {
      "fact": 2,
      "headline": "The Long Road to the Wall",
      "text": "Jon Snow watched the grey keep of Winterfell shrink behind him as he rode out of the gates. The air was thin and cold, the wind biting at his face. He shifted in his saddle, feeling the weight of the letter from Benjen Stark against his hip. Ghost, the pale direwolf, ran ahead of him, his paws striking the frozen earth with a soft, rhythmic thud. Six men of the household rode alongside, their faces grim, their eyes fixed on the distant grey line of the Wall.",
      "pov": "Jon Snow"
    },
    {
      "fact": 3,
      "headline": "The Towers of Moat Cailin",
      "text": "Robb Stark stood on the causeway, his hands white with the cold of the late summer air. The fallen stone was cleared, the path open and stark. The three standing towers, the eyes of the fort, were manned with archers, their arrows nocked and ready. The wind whistled through the gaps in the walls, a low, mournful sound. Robb looked south, toward the darkening horizon, but there was no word from the south, only the silence of the road and the watchful eyes of his men.",
      "pov": "Robb Stark"
    }
  ]
}

